//! spirvopt.zig -- a single-file Zig port of the SPIRV-Tools optimizer (source/opt, the library behind spirv-opt)
//!
//! Reads a SPIR-V binary, runs optimization passes on it, and writes the optimized binary:
//!
//!     zig build-exe spirvopt.zig -O ReleaseSafe          (Zig 0.17.1, standard library only)
//!     spirvopt in.spv -o out.spv                          (no pass flag: the -O pipeline)
//!     spirvopt --eliminate-dead-code-aggressive --ccp in.spv -o out.spv      (passes run in flag order)
//!     spirvopt --help
//!
//! Reference: SPIRV-Tools v2026.4 (2026-09-09), target environment 1.6, release (NDEBUG) build. The port aims at the
//! same output, byte for byte, as that spirv-opt for every pass flag; the differences are listed at the end of this
//! comment.
//!
//! What it does
//!   The program loads the binary into an in-memory IR (a Module of Block / Func / Inst objects), wraps it in a context
//!   (Ctx, the IRContext of the C++) that builds analyses on demand, runs the requested passes one after the other, and
//!   serializes the module again. A pass is a struct with a `run(ctx)` function that edits the IR through the context;
//!   when it ends, every analysis is dropped and the scratch memory is reset.
//!
//! Scope
//!   - Every pass of source/opt that spirv-opt exposes as a flag (all flags of `spirv-opt --help`, the -O / -Os /
//!     --legalize-hlsl presets, the --preserve-* options, -Oconfig=<file>), and the passes that only exist in the C++
//!     library API, with two flags added by this port:
//!         --interface-variable-scalar-replacement
//!         --eliminate-dead-output-stores=<consumer.spv>   (AnalyzeLiveInputPass runs on <consumer.spv> first)
//!     (AnalyzeLiveInputPass itself has no flag: it is the first half of the one above.)
//!   - Input: any module that spirv-val accepts. There is no validator: invalid modules are not diagnosed and may end
//!     in a fatal error (or, like the C++, in unspecified output).
//!   - Extended debug info (DebugInfo, OpenCL.DebugInfo.100, NonSemantic.Shader.DebugInfo) is not supported: the module
//!     is rejected, unless --strip-debug-ext removes those instructions while loading.
//!
//! Design (one file, flat code, no OOP / vtables)
//!   - No error unions: running out of memory and violated invariants are fatal ("spirvopt: error: ..." on stderr, exit
//!     status 1, nothing written). Every place where the C++ release build dereferences null, crashes or relies on
//!     undefined behaviour is fatal here instead; C++ asserts do nothing, as in an NDEBUG build.
//!   - Two arenas: the IR lives for the whole process; analyses and pass state live in a scratch arena that is reset
//!     after every pass, so no analysis survives the end of a pass and none is ever kept consistent across passes.
//!   - Passes are structs with `name`, `preserved` and `run(self, ctx) PassStatus`. PassSpec, a union with one field per
//!     pass, lists them, and the pass manager dispatches over it with `inline else`: there are no function pointers.
//!     The driver builds the list of passes from the command line, like opt.cpp.
//!   - The orders in which the C++ iterates its containers are reproduced where they reach the output: the bucket order
//!     of libstdc++'s unordered_set<uint32_t> (StdUSet32), std::set / std::map keyed by Instruction* (ordered by
//!     unique id), and the right-to-left evaluation of call arguments by g++ where it changes which id is allocated.
//!
//! Where the data structures differ from the C++ (for speed; the results are the same)
//!   - Ids are small dense integers, so def-use data are tables indexed by id (DefUseMgr) instead of hash maps and
//!     sets; the used-id lists of all instructions share one pool.
//!   - Folding rules and opcode predicates are comptime switches and comptime tables instead of tables of std::function.
//!   - Visitors take a context value and a comptime function, and the walks over users and uses of a definition are plain
//!     loops, so none of them goes through a function pointer or allocates a closure.
//!   - Containers that the C++ keeps as hash maps but never iterates are plain hash tables (`Map` / `Set`); where the
//!     order matters the code uses `OrderedMap` (insertion order), the sorted containers, or StdUSet32.
//!
//! Layout of the file
//!   The declarations are ordered by dependency: a top-level declaration only uses declarations that come before it,
//!   except where two declarations need each other. There are 61 such uses; they are:
//!     - the IR types: InstList, Inst, Block and Func point at each other;
//!     - Type and its Ty* variants (Type is the union of them, and they hold `*const Type`) and the helpers of that cycle;
//!     - Ctx and the analyses it owns (DecorMgr, Cfg, the dominator analyses, TypeMgr, ConstMgr, StructCfg,
//!       LoopDescriptor, ScalarEvolutionAnalysis, the liveness analyses): Ctx owns them and each keeps a `*Ctx`;
//!     - the same pairs elsewhere: Constant / ConstMgr, SENode / ScalarEvolutionAnalysis / SeSimplifier, RpCompute /
//!       RegisterLiveness, Fold and its rule tables, SsaPropagator / SpRunner;
//!     - a pass struct and the helper structs or functions that hold a pointer to it (`ctx: *AggressiveDce`, ...);
//!     - a few helper functions that call each other (`cpa...`, `ic...`, `inl...`, the flag parser of the driver).
//!   Declarations that do not depend on each other follow the order of the layers, from the bottom up. Each section
//!   starts with a comment that says what it contains; the sections, in this order, are:
//!     - the SPIR-V grammar tables (opcodes, operand kinds, enumerants, extended instruction sets);
//!     - runtime support: fatal / check, the two arenas, the containers List, Map, OrderedMap, Set, BitSet, Queue,
//!       SortedMap and SortedSet;
//!     - the operand and instruction model (Operand, Inst, InstList, Block, Func, Module), the binary parser, the IR
//!       loader and the binary writer;
//!     - the analyses (DefUseMgr, DecorMgr, FeatureMgr, Cfg, dominators), Ctx, the types (Type, TypeMgr), the constants
//!       (Constant, ConstMgr) and the instruction builder;
//!     - instruction folding (the folder, the constant folding rules, the folding rules) and the sparse propagation
//!       engine behind CCP;
//!     - the pass infrastructure and the passes: simple module passes, local memory passes, SSA rewriting, dead branch
//!       elimination, merge-return, inlining, aggressive DCE, CCP and simplification, redundancy elimination, access
//!       chain combining, vector DCE, dead insert elimination, load size reduction, if-conversion, array copy
//!       propagation, scalar replacement;
//!     - the loop infrastructure (Loop, LoopDescriptor), the loop unroller, loop utilities, scalar evolution, and the
//!       loop passes: invariant code motion, peeling, unswitching, fission, fusion, with the register pressure and loop
//!       dependence analyses they use;
//!     - the remaining passes: cfg-cleanup, dead members, fix-storage-class, legalization, canonicalize-ids,
//!       resolve-binding-conflicts, the descriptor and interface-variable passes, trim-capabilities,
//!       upgrade-memory-model, and the other small passes;
//!     - PassManager and the driver: command line, the -O / -Os / --legalize-hlsl recipes, file I/O, `main`.
//!
//! Conventions
//!   - Helpers that are not methods of a pass carry the initials of the pass as a prefix (`sr...` for
//!     ScalarReplacement, `cpa...` for CopyPropagateArrays, ...); functions that port a C++ member function keep the
//!     C++ name in lower camel case, and a comment names the C++ function when the port is not literal.
//!   - Walking the users or uses of a definition is a loop: `for (du.registeredUsers(def)) |user|` and
//!     `var uses = du.usesOf(def); while (uses.next()) |use|`. Do not re-analyze instructions during such a walk.
//!   - Every local has a written type (`const n: u32 = ...`), so the width of an integer is visible where it is used.
//!   - Arithmetic that wraps in the C++ (ids, hashes, constants) uses wrapping operators.
//!
//! Differences from spirv-opt (each is also noted where it occurs in the code)
//!   - Diagnostic text is not reproduced: messages go to stderr and the C++ message consumer is not emulated.
//!   - Failure paths: ID overflow is always fatal (the C++ reports a pass failure). Where the C++ dereferences a null
//!     pointer, crashes, kills an instruction twice or loops forever, this port is fatal: loop peeling / LICM / unswitch /
//!     fission / fusion on function declarations, a loop without a pre-header in unswitch / fission / fusion, null
//!     SENodes of the dependence analysis, Workaround1209 on declarations, the liveness passes with a duplicate
//!     kill-list entry, a variable shared by several entry points in the interface-variable scalar replacement,
//!     flatten-decorations where the C++ loops forever, INT64_MIN / -1, and the undefined behaviour of
//!     SimplifyRecurrentAddExpression.
//!   - Tolerance: DefUseMgr is fatal on a use of an undefined id (the C++ keeps going on some invalid modules); the
//!     TypeMgr is more lenient about unknown types in some invalid modules; DecorMgr ignores unexpected annotation
//!     opcodes; ScalarEvolution orders its pointer-keyed containers by unique id, which approximates the order of the
//!     heap addresses.
//!   - Not ported: the debug-info paths (DebugScope / DebugDeclare / DebugValue) of scalar replacement,
//!     copy-propagate-arrays and others, since extended debug info is rejected up front; the per-class register-pressure
//!     breakdown and SimulateFission; the validator (--target-env, --skip-validation and the validator flags are
//!     accepted and ignored; the target environment is fixed to 1.6).
//!   - interface-variable-scalar-replacement: the ids of the OpCompositeConstruct instructions created for a variable
//!     with three or more loads in one function can be numbered differently from spirv-opt, whose
//!     std::unordered_map<Instruction*, ...> iteration order depends on heap addresses. This port uses reverse
//!     insertion order, which is what libstdc++ produces unless two keys share a bucket. The instruction order and
//!     everything else is the same.

const std = @import("std");
const Allocator = std.mem.Allocator;

// BEGIN SPIR-V GRAMMAR TABLES: Op, the operand kinds, the enumerants and the extended instruction sets, taken from the grammar
// grammar: SPIR-V 1.6 revision 7, magic 0x07230203
pub const grammar_major: u32 = 1;
pub const grammar_minor: u32 = 6;
pub const grammar_revision: u32 = 7;

